name: dotnet-channels

Guide to `System.Threading.Channels`: thread-safe producer/consumer queues, back-pressure, `IAsyncEnumerable<T>`, and graceful shutdown. For hosted-service lifecycle and `BackgroundService`, see [skill:dotnet-background-services]; for async/cancellation patterns, see [skill:dotnet-csharp-async-patterns].

A `Channel<T>` has `ChannelWriter<T>` and `ChannelReader<T>` endpoints; writers produce and readers consume, with synchronization handled by the channel.

```csharp
Channel<WorkItem> channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<WorkItem>();
ChannelWriter<WorkItem> writer = channel.Writer;
ChannelReader<WorkItem> reader = channel.Reader;
```

| | Bounded | Unbounded |
|---|---|---|
| Create | `Channel.CreateBounded<T>(capacity)` | `Channel.CreateUnbounded<T>()` |
| Back-pressure | `FullMode` controls behavior when full | None; grows without limit |
| Memory | Capped at capacity | Can exhaust memory under load |
| Use | Production, untrusted producer rates | Guaranteed-low-volume, prototyping |

Unbounded -- use only when you control the producer rate.

```csharp
var bounded = Channel.CreateBounded<WorkItem>(
    new BoundedChannelOptions(capacity: 1000)
    { FullMode = BoundedChannelFullMode.Wait });
var unbounded = Channel.CreateUnbounded<WorkItem>();
```

`FullMode`: `Wait` blocks `WriteAsync` until space is available (default; reliable back-pressure); `DropOldest` discards oldest (telemetry); `DropNewest` discards newest (rate limiting); `DropWrite` discards attempted write and makes `TryWrite` return `false` (nonblocking overflow detection).

```csharp
var channel = Channel.CreateBounded(new BoundedChannelOptions(100)
{
    FullMode = BoundedChannelFullMode.DropOldest
}, itemDropped: (item, writer) =>
{
    logger.LogWarning("Dropped item due to channel overflow: {Id}", item.Id);
    (item as IDisposable)?.Dispose();
});
```

Since .NET 7, bounded drop modes accept an `itemDropped` callback receiving the dropped item and `ChannelWriter<T>`, useful for metrics, logging, cleanup, or rerouting.

```csharp
await writer.WriteAsync(item, cancellationToken);
if (!writer.TryWrite(item)) { /* Handle overflow */ }
```

Multiple producers can call `WriteAsync` or `TryWrite` concurrently without external locking.

Call `Complete()` or `TryComplete()` when no more items will be produced; consumers can then detect completion. `TryComplete()` is idempotent. To propagate errors: `writer.TryComplete(new InvalidOperationException("Source failed"));`

Preferred consumer pattern drains available items before awaiting again, reducing async state machine overhead:

```csharp
while (await reader.WaitToReadAsync(cancellationToken))
    while (reader.TryRead(out var item))
        await ProcessAsync(item, cancellationToken);
```

Simpler alternative:

```csharp
var item = await reader.ReadAsync(cancellationToken);
await ProcessAsync(item, cancellationToken);
```

Scale with multiple consumer tasks; each item is read by exactly one consumer. Hosted-service consumers can run `ConsumeAsync` workers under `Task.WhenAll`, use `stoppingToken`, create a DI scope per item, and log processing errors.

`ChannelReader<T>.ReadAllAsync()` returns `IAsyncEnumerable<T>` and completes after writer completion and drain:

```csharp
await foreach (var item in reader.ReadAllAsync(cancellationToken))
    await ProcessAsync(item, cancellationToken);
```

ASP.NET Core streaming:

```csharp
app.MapGet("/api/events/stream", (
    ChannelReader<ServerEvent> reader, CancellationToken ct) =>
    reader.ReadAllAsync(ct));
```

With the `System.Linq.Async` NuGet package, compose streams with async LINQ, e.g. `.Where(...).Buffer(50)`.

For adapting callback/feed producers, write updates to a channel, call `TryComplete()` on success, and `TryComplete(ex)` on failure; `ReadAllAsync(ct)` propagates the error.

`SingleReader = true` or `SingleWriter = true` enables lock-free optimizations. The channel trusts these hints -- violating them (multiple concurrent readers when `SingleReader = true`) causes data corruption.

See [references/detailed-examples.md](references/detailed-examples.md) for complete samples and advanced patterns.
